#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prime a fresh checkout of parlor.
#
# Five commands on purpose: install EXACTLY the committed lockfile (`npm ci`,
# which deletes node_modules and refuses to drift), run the full suite, PROVE
# THE TREE STILL TYPE CHECKS, PROVE THE TREE STILL BUILDS, then check that the
# tree still says what CI assumes about it. A green `bin/prime` means the
# checkout reproduces AND the gate is the gate. Run it before touching code,
# and again before handing work off.
#
# WHY `npm run build` IS HERE AND WAS NOT, which cost a fork months.
#
# The suite cannot see this class of error at all. `npm test` is vitest: it
# transpiles each file it loads and hands it to a runtime, in jsdom, one module
# at a time. Nothing in it resolves a relative URL inside a stylesheet, walks
# the module graph a bundler walks, or produces the `dist/` that
# `server/index.mjs` loads — so the suite is green on a tree that does not
# bundle.
#
# This tree has the receipts, and they are written down where the build config
# lives. `vite.config.mts`'s `copyFontFiles` header records a build that printed
# sixteen lines about a `./files/geist-*.woff2` it had not resolved, emitted a
# stylesheet pointing at a directory the build never creates, and left a 404 on
# every font request — while every page rendered, every route answered and
# `/healthz` was green. `find dist/client -name '*.woff2'` returned nothing. The
# build is the only command in this repository that sees that, and it saw it as
# a warning rather than a failure, which is the argument for running it on every
# commit instead of in a job nobody opens.
#
# WHAT THE BUILD IS *NO LONGER* PROVING, STATED HERE SO NOBODY INHERITS IT.
# The paragraph this replaced said the build was here because
# `src/components/ui/feedback.tsx` once called `useCallback`/`useEffect`/
# `useId`/`useRef` with no `"use client"` directive, and that only `next build`
# could catch that. Both halves are history now. The file is hook-free —
# `ConfirmDialog` lives in `./confirm-dialog`, carrying the directive with it —
# and the build is `vite build`, which enforces no server/client split, so a
# missing directive is not an error it is able to raise. What catches that class
# of error today is a source scan, and it is in the suite:
# `src/components/ui/client-boundary.test.ts`, which reads every `.tsx` under
# `src`. Its own header is where the reasoning for a scan rather than a compiler
# is written down; start there before changing anything under it.
#
# CI DID catch it — there is a `typecheck + build` job and it went red. That is
# not the same as catching it. cafaye/site forked this template and inherited
# the trap; its local gate was green, its CI build job was red, and it stayed
# that way for months because a developer who trusts the gate they can run has
# no reason to open a browser tab and read CI. A check that only runs on a
# runner is a check its author watches fail from a distance.
#
# So the build is in the gate a developer runs. It costs a few seconds
# (`npm run build` measured at 5.2s wall on this tree, 150ms of it vite's own),
# and it is the difference between "the local gate is green" and "the tree
# bundles".
#
# WHY `npm run typecheck` IS HERE, which is the same argument one step earlier.
#
# `npm test` cannot see a type error either, and for a different reason than the
# build: vitest transpiles each file it loads and hands it to a runtime. A call
# with three arguments where the signature takes two, a property that does not
# exist on a type, an interface implemented with the wrong member — none of them
# stop the module from executing, so the suite passes and the runner rejects the
# tree. `tsc --noEmit` is the only thing in this repository that reads a
# declaration instead of an expression.
#
# Cafaye/site — the fork built from this template — carried 29 type errors
# behind a green local gate for as long as the migration ran. Nothing about the
# 29 was subtle: they were ordinary drift accumulated by a tree whose only
# compiler ran in a job nobody opens unless they are already suspicious. The
# cost of leaving the check on a runner is not the seconds it takes to run it.
# It is that a developer who trusts the gate they can run has no reason to look,
# so the errors sit there for weeks.
#
# So `tsc` is in the gate a developer runs, in front of the build because it is
# the cheaper and more specific of the two: a type error should be a sentence
# about a file and a line, not a bundler error about the same thing.
#
# `tests/validate-ci.sh` is here rather than only in the workflow on purpose: it
# is the check that the runtime pin, the lockfile guard and the gate command
# have not drifted apart, and a check that only runs on a CI runner is a check
# its author never watched run. It is offline, needs no tool but node and grep,
# and takes well under a second.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

echo "==> npm ci"
npm ci

echo "==> npm test"
npm test

echo "==> npm run typecheck"
npm run typecheck

echo "==> npm run build"
npm run build

echo "==> tests/validate-ci.sh --self-test"
bash tests/validate-ci.sh --self-test
